    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d286">

                <pb facs="0375" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6166: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-04-11T18:00:00+09:00">April 11, 1933—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 11—6:37 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>75. My 67, March 25, 2 p.m. The limited objective attack referred to has been
                    approved. According to statement of Japanese General Staff to Military Attaché
                    the attack was launched April 10 by Japanese line from Kupeikow to Shanhaikwan
                    in order to dislodge Chinese from commanding positions immediately south of
                    Great Wall, and there establish Japanese outpost line of resistance to cover
                    main line of resistance along Wall. No advance in force into North China
                    contemplated.</p>
                <p>The situation on the Manchuria-Soviet border occasioned by dispute over Chinese
                    Eastern Railway rolling stock appears to be somewhat <pb facs="0376" n="274"
                        xml:id="pg274"/> tense but the Japanese General Staff state that they do not
                    expect it to develop into a serious armed clash. Not repeated to Peiping as
                    above information considered confidential by Japanese military authorities.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
